<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zapotec civilization</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zapotecs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zapotec civilization arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where from about 500 BCE Monte Albán became one of the first cities and states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a> developed one of the earliest writing systems in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, built temple and palace complexes and buried their nobles in painted tombs. After Monte Albán declined they lived in smaller kingdoms such as Zaachila, Mitla and Tehuantepec, and Zapotec languages are still spoken today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zapotec-civilization.jpg" alt="Zapotec civilization" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yavidaxiu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zapotecos.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Zapotec civilization arose in the Central Valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, three broad valleys, Etla in the west, Ocotlán in the south and Mitla in the east, that meet about 200 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. The land suited agriculture and is considered one of the cradles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>. The three valleys held three societies separated by a no-man's-land, and burned temples and sacrificed war captives suggest that they competed. At the end of the Rosario phase, 700 to 500 BCE, the largest settlement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote">San José Mogote</a>, lost most of its population.</p><p>At the same time a new settlement grew on a mountain in the no-man's-land: Monte Albán. Its early pottery resembles that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote">San José Mogote</a>, which suggests settlers came from there. Walls and fortifications of the Monte Albán 2 phase, about 100 BCE to 200 CE, suggest that the city was built in response to a military threat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The state formed at Monte Albán began to expand in the late Monte Albán 1 phase, 400 to 100 BCE, and grew through Monte Albán 2. Zapotec rulers took control of provinces outside the valley, and by 200 CE their influence reached from Quiotepec in the north to Ocelotepec and Chiltepec in the south. Local pottery styles outside the valley were suddenly replaced by Zapotec ones. Marcus and Flannery see both colonisation and conquest. Monte Albán remained the largest city of the southern Mexican highlands until about 700.</p><p>Aztec forces conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a> in 1458. The last battle, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guiengola">Guiengola</a>, was fought between 1497 and 1502. When news of the Aztec defeat came, King Cosijoeza ordered his people not to confront the Spaniards, and sent a delegation to seek an alliance. Francisco de Orozco arrived on 25 November 1521 and the valley submitted, with some resistance at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antequera">Antequera</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a> lived in villages and towns in stone-and-mortar houses. They developed a calendar and a glyphic writing system, which is read in columns from top to bottom and remains undeciphered. The earliest known Zapotec writing is Monument 3, a Danzante stone from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote">San José Mogote</a>, though its dating is doubted. Their religion was polytheistic: Cocijo was the rain god and Pitao Cozobi the god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and elite families believed they descended from beings who lived among the clouds, hence the name Be'ena'a, the Cloud People. The Zapotec languages belong to the Oto-Manguean family and are tonal, and are still spoken.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zapotec civilization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some compounds were occupied by migrants: the Tlailotlacan district on the western edge housed people from the Valley of Oaxaca who kept <b>Zapotec-style</b> tombs and pottery for centuries, and the so-called Merchants’ Barrio in the east contained pottery from the Gulf Coast and the Maya lowlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Mitla, Lyobaa in <b>Zapotec</b>, was occupied for many centuries, but it grew into a major centre after the decline of Monte Albán, from around 900.</blockquote><a href="/state/mitla">Mitla</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Danzantes carvings show slain or sacrificed captives, and Building J lists conquered places, among the earliest evidence of <b>Zapotec</b> writing and expansion.</blockquote><a href="/state/monte-alban">Monte Albán</a></li><li><blockquote>Zaachila, called Teozapotlan by the Nahuas, emerged as the main <b>Zapotec</b> royal seat in the Valley of Oaxaca in the Postclassic period, from around 1100, filling the gap left by the decline of Monte Albán.</blockquote><a href="/state/zaachila">Zaachila</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 15th century the <b>Zapotec</b> king Cocijoeza of Zaachila conquered the Isthmus of Tehuantepec from Mixe, Zoque and Huave peoples and moved his royal seat there, around 1490, gaining control of the trade route between central Mexico and Soconusco.</blockquote><a href="/state/zapotec-kingdom-of-tehuantepec">Zapotec Kingdom of Tehuantepec</a></li></ul></section>
